<p>Path Finder developer Cocoatech has been at this a long time. Twenty-five years, in fact. To celebrate its anniversary and the launch of its new website, the company has also <a href="https://cocoatech.io/post/path-finder-turns-25-a-new-website-and-some-special-guests/">generously decided to spotlight some other longtime Mac developers</a>:</p>
<blockquote><p>
  You can’t have a proper anniversary party without guests, so for our anniversary, we’ve invited five fellow independent Mac developers to join the celebration:</p>
<p>  Our guests are <a href="https://cocoatech.io/post/devontechnologies-twenty-four-years-of-finding-connections/">DEVONtechnologies</a>, <a href="https://cocoatech.io/post/c-command-software-twenty-five-years-of-making-the-mac-more-useful/">C-Command Software</a>, <a href="https://cocoatech.io/post/st-clair-software-nearly-forty-years-of-making-everyday-mac-tasks-easier/">St. Clair Software</a>, <a href="https://cocoatech.io/post/shirt-pocket-twenty-two-years-of-making-backups-less-frightening/">Shirt Pocket</a>, and <a href="https://cocoatech.io/post/stairways-software-thirty-one-years-of-making-the-mac-do-more/">Stairways Software</a>. We’ll be sharing the stories behind their applications, how they got started, and how their software has changed over the years here on this blog. We’ll finish the history series with a <a href="https://cocoatech.io/post/path-finder-25-years-of-making-the-mac-our-own/">look back at Path Finder’s own history</a>.
</p></blockquote>
<p>I use or have used apps from pretty much all these developers. Several of them remain indispensable to my workflow to this day. Every item in the series is linked above (on this Six Colors post, not the original) and can also be found <a href="https://cocoatech.io/author/alexandra/">on this Path Finder archive page</a>.</p>
